---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';


const path = "/compare/marsello-vs-yotpo-email-sms";
const title = "Marsello after Yotpo Email & SMS: Shopify decisions";
const desc = "Evaluate Marsello when retail loyalty and POS transactions must drive messaging after Yotpo messaging retirement. Decide separately whether to retain Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty, and which ledger owns points.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I renew Yotpo Email & SMS instead?",
    "answer": "The messaging service is retired. Do not budget for a renewed Yotpo sender or assume the old dashboard can supply an export. Reviews and Loyalty are separate continuing products."
  },
  {
    "question": "When is Marsello a useful candidate?",
    "answer": "When your requirements include loyalty activity across supported POS and ecommerce connections alongside email or SMS. Demonstrate your exact connector, redemption and return workflow before committing."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I keep Yotpo Reviews?",
    "answer": "Yes, evaluate Reviews separately from messaging and points. Identify its request sender and verify any review-status data needed in the receiving stack. Retaining Reviews does not establish that a Marsello connector supplies every field."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should both loyalty ledgers remain active?",
    "answer": "Avoid treating two balances as equally authoritative. Either retain Yotpo Loyalty as the ledger with verified event delivery to messaging, or scope a separate loyalty move with opening balances, liabilities and reversal reconciliation."
  },
  {
    "question": "What settles the POS decision?",
    "answer": "A staff-controlled purchase, online redemption, partial return and delayed replay must reconcile to one balance and one eligible message. Record connector behavior and the associate steps required to resolve a mismatch."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare cost?",
    "answer": "Quote twelve months of contacts, sends, locations, orders and SMS destinations, plus retained modules, setup and staff reconciliation. Count outstanding rewards as liabilities separately from software fees."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I recover old Yotpo messaging records?",
    "answer": "Inventory saved files and ask support what remains accessible. Validate permission history and suppressions before any import; a promised automated export or retired-sender rollback is not a migration plan."
  },
  {
    "question": "Were these merchant exercises performed?",
    "answer": "No. All merchant numbers and acceptance cases below are proposed fixtures. No supplier ranking, observed revenue lift or measured migration outcome is asserted."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="catalog-kicker">Migration and buying decision</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Evaluate Marsello when retail loyalty and POS transactions must drive messaging after Yotpo messaging retirement. Decide separately whether to retain Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty, and which ledger owns points.</p></header>
    <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl"><TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Retail loyalty candidate", "tool": "Marsello", "reason": "Demonstrate supported POS and ecommerce earn, redeem and reversal paths."}, {"label": "Existing review program", "tool": "Yotpo Reviews", "reason": "Retain if its separately evaluated collection and display jobs remain useful."}, {"label": "Points authority", "tool": "One ledger", "reason": "Decide ownership before enabling points-triggered marketing."}]} /></div>
    <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Marsello receiving stack</th><th>Existing Yotpo services</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Messaging</td><td>Evaluate current email/SMS offer and destination coverage</td><td>Email/SMS retired</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Loyalty</td><td>Candidate ledger only after reconciliation</td><td>Loyalty retention is a separate decision</td></tr>
      <tr><td>POS events</td><td>Prove exact connector and return handling</td><td>Existing balances do not prove receiving event compatibility</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Annual scope</td><td>Quote locations, usage and implementation</td><td>Include only retained Reviews/Loyalty services</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Messaging retirement changes the purchase</h2>
      <p>Yotpo announced full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. Its messaging login now reports that the SMS dashboard and background services are decommissioned. Reviews and Loyalty continue as separate products; keeping them does not retain a messaging sender. See the <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">Yotpo sunset announcement</a> and <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">decommission notice</a>. This page evaluates a receiving stack, not a new Yotpo messaging purchase.</p>
      <h2>Choose the ledger before the sender</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://www.marsello.com/">Marsello product overview</a> describes loyalty, email/SMS campaigns and automation connected to POS and ecommerce. These are reasons to evaluate a retail workflow, not evidence of a particular connector passing your return rules. Ask for a demonstration using your POS version, locations and Shopify setup.</p>
      <p>Write two possible designs. In one, Marsello owns loyalty and messaging while Yotpo Reviews keeps collection and display. In the other, Yotpo Loyalty remains authoritative and a receiving sender consumes only verified events. For the second design, require evidence of the actual field mapping, latency, retries and connector cost; do not infer interoperability from both products having points features.</p>
      <p>If points move, reconcile opening balances and outstanding reward liability by customer and store before any earn rules activate. Keep an audit reference for adjustments and associate overrides. A messaging migration alone should not silently change redemption terms or award points twice.</p>
      <h2>Retail acceptance: vinyl allocation and outdoor returns</h2>
      <p>All six merchant baselines here are proposed inputs, not businesses we tested. For a vinyl shop at 1,800 orders and $72k monthly revenue, use 200–400-unit pressings around Record Store Day. Have an associate reserve a VIP copy, then redeem online. Accept the design only when the allocation and points reservation are visible to the responsible staff member and a later cancellation releases each exactly once. Education about grading can continue without a discount on scarce stock.</p>
      <p>For outdoor retail at 2,400 orders and $112k monthly revenue, run separate ultralight and family-camping interests before Memorial Day. Complete a POS tent purchase, queue a loyalty follow-up, then partially return the basket online. Verify that the remaining qualifying spend determines the balance and audience; a return must not leave an obsolete tier-upgrade email waiting. Record event IDs and arrival times to distinguish a connector delay from a bad earning rule.</p>
      <h2>Preorders: keyboard close and board-game fulfillment</h2>
      <p>Use 940 keyboard orders and $58k monthly revenue with six-week group buys on $180–$420 kits and education across five product pages. The revenue/order baseline is about $61.70, so it cannot represent every order containing a full kit; model accessories and deposits separately. At group-buy close, disable recovery for closed kits while retaining permitted switch education. Replay a deposit event after the balance payment and confirm no second full-value points award.</p>
      <p>For 1,050 board-game orders and $67k monthly revenue before Essen, allocate part of a preorder and ship only the available title. Decide whether points become spendable on payment or shipment and write that rule down. Verify the remaining unshipped line stays out of winback, while the fulfilled line can enter its own education path. Assign one sender for review requests so keeping Yotpo Reviews does not create a second request.</p>
      <h2>Replenishment: tea gifts and coffee pauses</h2>
      <p>For tea at 720 orders and $44k monthly revenue, use 28–35-day replenishment windows by blend. Before the Q4 gift-box peak, separate buyer, recipient and subscriber. A gift recipient must not inherit the buyer’s points identity or marketing permission. Test a subscription skip and a gift return, then check both the next reminder and the reward balance.</p>
      <p>For coffee at 860 orders and $52k monthly revenue, start with day-thirty roast-date replenishment and separate wholesale samples. Pause a subscription after queueing a reminder, then merge a duplicate POS customer. Acceptance requires the pause to remain effective and the merge to preserve one ledger with traceable adjustments. Show associates how to find the surviving profile without enrolling the customer again.</p>
      <h2>Cutover with accountable event ownership</h2>
      <p>Inventory saved messaging definitions, permissions, suppressions and balances before importing. The retired sender is not a fallback. Resolve inaccessible records with support, and hold records with unknown permission out of promotional enrollment. Keep channel and purpose evidence separate from purchase history.</p>
      <p>For each purchase, refund, points correction and review event, record its authority, stable identifier, effective time and receiving job. Demonstrate delayed delivery, a repeated event and an out-of-order correction. Accept only when stale points reminders cancel, marketing eligibility is checked before dispatch and operational receipts remain with their designated sender. Measure staff reconciliation minutes during the exercise.</p>
      <h2>Price the program and make the decision</h2>
      <p>At a proposed 12,000–18,000 contacts, obtain current written quotes for the same locations, orders, sends and SMS destinations. Add retained Yotpo modules, connector upkeep, onboarding, rewards accounting and associate training. Run a waitlist import at flat orders and a peak broadcast month as separate sensitivities. Do not assume a historical starting price still applies.</p>
      <p>For fixed-goods economics, propose $1,000 list-price sales, $100 discounts and $400 unchanged goods cost: contribution before software and labor is $500. An additional $20 message cost leaves $480. Do not reduce goods cost because a discount reduced revenue; add reward redemption cost once under the agreed accounting rule. A holdout can estimate incremental contribution with uncertainty; attributed sales alone cannot.</p>
      <p>Buy Marsello when the demonstrated retail ledger and messaging workflow meets your requirements at an acceptable annual cost. Retain Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty where its separate job remains useful, with a proven event boundary. If your points ledger is already settled and retail coordination is irrelevant, compare other active senders rather than buying another loyalty program for consolidation alone.</p>
      <p>Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">selection framework</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">pricing worksheet</a> to record acceptance evidence and contract scope.</p>
    </article>
  </div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Marsello after Yotpo messaging FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
